WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.400
Block two.

2
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:05.025
Not what a database stores, but who stands behind it.

3
00:00:05.625 --> 00:00:11.100
Here is the distinction this whole block rests on, and it is subtler than it first appears.

4
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:16.375
These four words describe *sources of a claim*, not kinds of record.

5
00:00:16.375 --> 00:00:20.400
That matters, because one record routinely mixes them.

6
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:28.425
A single Swiss-Prot entry contains curated statements, experimental observations and predicted features on the same page.

7
00:00:28.425 --> 00:00:33.025
A PDB entry is both submitted and experimental at once.

8
00:00:33.025 --> 00:00:35.350
Taking them one at a time.

9
00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:39.000
Submitted means INSDC as deposited.

10
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.450
Somebody sent this in, and it is stored as sent.

11
00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:49.975
Quality varies enormously, and there is a great deal of redundancy, because nothing is removed when something better arrives.

12
00:00:49.975 --> 00:00:55.950
Predicted means a program inferred it, from a genome or from similarity to something else.

13
00:00:55.950 --> 00:01:01.450
Curated means staff read the evidence, weighed it, and merged records accordingly.

14
00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:06.750
That is expensive, which is why there is so much less of it than of the alternatives.

15
00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.825
Experimental means somebody measured it.

16
00:01:09.825 --> 00:01:15.550
The summary sentence: databases differ less in what they store than in who vouches for it.

17
00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:20.550
When you open an unfamiliar database, that is the question to ask first.

18
00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:27.400
A word about why curation is rarer than you might expect, since it is obviously the most reliable of the four.

19
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:32.375
Curation is a person reading the literature and deciding what it supports.

20
00:01:32.375 --> 00:01:40.425
That takes an expert an appreciable amount of time per entry, and the number of experts does not grow when the number of sequences does.

21
00:01:40.425 --> 00:01:47.250
Swiss-Prot has been curated continuously since nineteen eighty-six and holds a few hundred thousand entries.

22
00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:51.625
In the same period, sequencing has produced hundreds of millions.

23
00:01:51.625 --> 00:01:56.775
So the fraction of our data that anybody has read is falling, and will keep falling.

24
00:01:56.775 --> 00:02:00.875
That is not a criticism of anyone; it is arithmetic.

25
00:02:00.875 --> 00:02:08.650
It does mean that "somebody has checked this" is an increasingly strong claim about a record, and worth noticing when it is true.

26
00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:11.575
Four claims on the slide.

27
00:02:11.575 --> 00:02:15.750
For each one, decide which of the four sources stands behind it.

28
00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:20.000
The sequence of that predicted protein record: predicted.

29
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.600
And the record says so — MODEL REFSEQ.

30
00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:29.325
The atom positions in two H H B: experimental, from X-ray diffraction.

31
00:02:29.325 --> 00:02:34.725
But also submitted, because the depositors sent in the coordinates they refined.

32
00:02:34.725 --> 00:02:37.425
Both labels are correct at once.

33
00:02:37.425 --> 00:02:42.225
The function text in the Swiss-Prot entry for beta-globin: curated.

34
00:02:42.225 --> 00:02:47.925
A person read the literature and wrote that sentence, and there is a citation attached to it.

35
00:02:47.925 --> 00:02:52.725
A sequence as deposited in ENA: submitted, and nothing more.

36
00:02:52.725 --> 00:02:56.600
Nobody has vouched for it beyond the person who sent it.

37
00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:00.975
The second one is the interesting case, and I want to underline it.

38
00:03:00.975 --> 00:03:05.550
A record being experimental does not stop it being submitted.

39
00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:10.375
These categories overlap, and a claim can have more than one kind of backing.

40
00:03:10.375 --> 00:03:18.150
Asking "which one is it?" is the wrong question; asking "what is behind this, and how much does that support it?" is the right one.

41
00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:25.525
UniProt's knowledgebase has two sections, and the difference between them matters every single time you use it.

42
00:03:25.525 --> 00:03:28.225
Swiss-Prot is the reviewed section.

43
00:03:28.225 --> 00:03:35.000
Curators check the function, the domains and the literature, and there is exactly one entry per protein.

44
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:39.125
If two submissions describe the same protein, they are merged.

45
00:03:39.125 --> 00:03:42.300
TrEMBL is the unreviewed section.

46
00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:49.125
Entries are annotated automatically, and most of them are translations of predicted coding sequences.

47
00:03:49.125 --> 00:03:51.325
Nobody has read them.

48
00:03:51.325 --> 00:03:56.600
One thing people consistently get wrong: these are not a subset and a superset.

49
00:03:56.600 --> 00:04:00.175
An entry sits in one or the other, never both.

50
00:04:00.175 --> 00:04:04.775
When a TrEMBL entry is reviewed, it *moves* into Swiss-Prot.

51
00:04:04.775 --> 00:04:07.925
And the sizes are not remotely comparable.

52
00:04:07.925 --> 00:04:13.550
Reviewed entries number in the hundreds of thousands; unreviewed in the hundreds of millions.

53
00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:23.475
That ratio has only ever moved in one direction since sequencing became cheap, and it will keep moving, because curation is done by people and sequencing is not.

54
00:04:23.475 --> 00:04:29.125
So when somebody says "it is in UniProt", the useful follow-up question is: which part?

55
00:04:29.725 --> 00:04:40.950
UniProt also grades how confident it is that the protein exists at all — which is a question most people never think to ask, and which has a surprising number of interesting answers.

56
00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:44.600
Every entry carries a protein existence level.

57
00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:53.175
Level one means protein-level evidence: somebody has detected the protein itself, by mass spectrometry or antibody or structure.

58
00:04:53.175 --> 00:04:59.100
Level two means transcript-level evidence — the RNA was seen, the protein inferred.

59
00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:02.225
Level three is inferred from homology.

60
00:05:02.225 --> 00:05:04.775
Level four is predicted.

61
00:05:04.775 --> 00:05:07.375
Level five is uncertain.

62
00:05:07.375 --> 00:05:15.525
Beta-globin is level one, as you would hope for a protein that has been studied since the nineteen-fifties and crystallised hundreds of times.

63
00:05:15.525 --> 00:05:24.975
But for a protein from a newly sequenced genome, the level may well be four, and then the word "protein" in that record is doing a lot of optimistic work.

64
00:05:24.975 --> 00:05:29.925
It means: a gene-finding program thinks there is an open reading frame here.

65
00:05:29.925 --> 00:05:32.425
Nobody has seen the product.

66
00:05:32.425 --> 00:05:35.175
Why does this matter in practice?

67
00:05:35.175 --> 00:05:38.950
Because the existence level is a filter you can use.

68
00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:50.625
If you are building a reference set of proteins — to train something, to count something, to compare organisms — including level four and five entries means including things that may not exist.

69
00:05:50.625 --> 00:05:53.300
For some questions that is fine.

70
00:05:53.300 --> 00:06:03.750
For others it will systematically inflate your numbers, in a way that correlates with how well studied the organism is, which is exactly the kind of bias that is hard to see afterwards.

71
00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:06.625
UniProt lets you filter on it.

72
00:06:06.625 --> 00:06:09.950
Most people never do, and most people should.

73
00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:14.450
If you have a protein and one question about it, start here.

74
00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:33.350
The Swiss-Prot entry for beta-globin — accession P six eight eight seven one — gives you the function, the disease variants including sickle-cell anaemia, the post-translational modifications, the sequence features, and links out to essentially everything else: the gene, the transcripts, the structures, the families, the interactions.

75
00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:40.625
That makes it the best single entry point for a protein-level question, and it is where I would start nine times out of ten.

76
00:06:40.625 --> 00:06:43.425
One component we have not mentioned.

77
00:06:43.425 --> 00:06:52.600
UniProt has a third part, UniParc, which stores every unique protein sequence exactly once, under an identifier that is never reused.

78
00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:55.125
It is not somewhere you browse.

79
00:06:55.125 --> 00:07:04.375
It is how you recognise that a record in one database and a record in another hold literally the same sequence, even when neither of them cites the other.

80
00:07:04.375 --> 00:07:12.300
One more feature worth knowing about, because it saves an enormous amount of time: the evidence attached to individual statements.

81
00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:16.875
A Swiss-Prot entry does not simply assert that a protein does something.

82
00:07:16.875 --> 00:07:25.050
Beside each annotation there is a note of where that came from — a publication, an experiment, a similarity, an automatic rule.

83
00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:27.575
You can click through to the paper.

84
00:07:27.575 --> 00:07:32.400
So when you need to cite a protein's function, you do not cite the database.

85
00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:38.725
You follow the evidence to the paper and cite that, having checked that it says what the annotation claims.

86
00:07:38.725 --> 00:07:45.350
That is the difference between using a database and quoting it, and it is the habit this whole block is aiming at.

87
00:07:45.950 --> 00:07:51.300
Protein families are hierarchical, and InterPro is where you see the hierarchy.

88
00:07:51.300 --> 00:08:00.375
For beta-globin there are five nested entries, running from the globin superfamily at the top down to the beta-type globins specifically at the bottom.

89
00:08:00.375 --> 00:08:09.175
Which level you want depends entirely on your question — "is this a globin?" and "is this a beta-globin?" are different questions with different answers.

90
00:08:09.175 --> 00:08:21.325
The signatures come from about a dozen member databases, and InterPro's job is to reconcile them: where several member databases describe the same family, InterPro merges them into one entry.

91
00:08:21.325 --> 00:08:30.200
Most of those signatures are profile hidden Markov models, which is Session five's subject, so you will understand what is underneath them in two weeks.

92
00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:34.875
A piece of recent history worth knowing, because old links are everywhere.

93
00:08:34.875 --> 00:08:40.350
Pfam's own website was retired in twenty twenty-three and merged into InterPro.

94
00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:43.400
If you have a bookmark, it now sends you here.

95
00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:56.300
And for non-coding RNA, Rfam plays the equivalent role, using covariance models — which score base pairing together with the sequence, because for RNA the structure is often more conserved than the bases.

96
00:08:56.300 --> 00:09:00.250
Beta-globin has no Rfam entry, being a protein.

97
00:09:00.250 --> 00:09:07.450
Rfam's transfer RNA family is R F zero zero zero zero five, if you want to see what one looks like.

98
00:09:08.050 --> 00:09:12.750
A structure, and a number that describes the experiment rather than the model.

99
00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:22.000
Two H H B is human deoxyhaemoglobin, determined by X-ray diffraction, at a resolution of one point seven four ångström.

100
00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:24.950
Smaller numbers mean finer detail.

101
00:09:24.950 --> 00:09:29.725
Now, resolution is widely misunderstood, so let me be precise.

102
00:09:29.725 --> 00:09:36.325
It describes the *data*: how much detail the diffraction measurements could resolve.

103
00:09:36.325 --> 00:09:41.900
It does not describe the correctness of the atomic model that was built into those data.

104
00:09:41.900 --> 00:09:56.775
A model at one point seven four ångström is built on good data, and it can still have errors in it — particularly in flexible regions, in side chains at the surface, and in anything the crystallographer had to interpret rather than read off.

105
00:09:56.775 --> 00:10:01.175
So resolution tells you what was possible, not what was achieved.

106
00:10:01.175 --> 00:10:07.950
Beta-globin appears in hundreds of PDB entries, at various resolutions and in various states.

107
00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:17.175
Session six treats coordinates like these as data to be analysed, which is the right frame of mind: they are measurements with uncertainty, not facts.

108
00:10:17.175 --> 00:10:22.725
Two more things about a PDB entry that are easy to miss and change how you read it.

109
00:10:22.725 --> 00:10:29.375
The first is that what is deposited is a *model* fitted to data, plus the data themselves.

110
00:10:29.375 --> 00:10:32.300
The coordinates are an interpretation.

111
00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:38.575
For most of a well-resolved structure that interpretation is not in doubt; at the edges it can be.

112
00:10:38.575 --> 00:10:40.950
The second is the crystal.

113
00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:50.825
A protein in a crystal is at high concentration, often at low temperature, frequently with additives that helped it crystallise, and packed against copies of itself.

114
00:10:50.825 --> 00:10:53.750
Most of the time this does not matter.

115
00:10:53.750 --> 00:11:01.825
Occasionally it does — a loop is held in place by a neighbouring molecule, or a conformation is selected that is rare in solution.

116
00:11:01.825 --> 00:11:11.550
So a structure is a snapshot of one state under one set of conditions, which is exactly the point Session six returns to with haemoglobin's T and R forms.

117
00:11:12.150 --> 00:11:16.325
And here is the same protein, predicted rather than measured.

118
00:11:16.325 --> 00:11:25.000
The AlphaFold database entry carries a confidence score called pLDDT, given per residue on a scale from zero to a hundred.

119
00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:30.200
It is the model's own estimate of how right it is about each part of the structure.

120
00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:35.950
The bands are worth memorising, because you will use them constantly from Session twelve onwards.

121
00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:40.475
Above ninety, the model is accurate down to the side-chain positions.

122
00:11:40.475 --> 00:11:45.025
Above seventy, the backbone is usually right even if details are not.

123
00:11:45.025 --> 00:11:55.200
Below fifty, confidence is very low — and one common reason for that is intrinsic disorder, where the protein may have no single structure for the model to be right about.

124
00:11:55.200 --> 00:12:02.200
A low pLDDT is sometimes a correct statement about biology rather than a failure of prediction.

125
00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:07.000
AlphaFold DB covers essentially every protein in UniProt.

126
00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:11.350
That is a sentence that would have been science fiction in twenty nineteen.

127
00:12:11.950 --> 00:12:16.900
Both are numbers attached to a structure, and they mean entirely different things.

128
00:12:16.900 --> 00:12:22.300
I labour this because the two get put side by side in tables, and they should not be.

129
00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:28.575
Resolution is a property of experimental data — a statement about an instrument and a crystal.

130
00:12:28.575 --> 00:12:35.250
pLDDT is a model's opinion of itself — a statement about a neural network's confidence.

131
00:12:35.250 --> 00:12:42.225
They are not comparable, they are not convertible, and a structure with a good pLDDT has not been measured at all.

132
00:12:42.225 --> 00:12:50.625
For beta-globin, with hundreds of experimental structures available, the predicted model adds very little; you would use the experiment.

133
00:12:50.625 --> 00:12:57.925
AlphaFold DB matters most for the enormous number of proteins that have no structure at all, which is most of them.

134
00:12:57.925 --> 00:13:05.725
There is a third number you will meet alongside these, and it is worth naming now so it does not confuse you later: the B-factor.

135
00:13:05.725 --> 00:13:12.775
In an experimental structure it describes how much an atom moves, or how poorly it is pinned down by the data.

136
00:13:12.775 --> 00:13:19.500
In an AlphaFold model, the field in the file that would hold the B-factor holds the pLDDT instead.

137
00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:26.350
So the same column of the same file format means two unrelated things depending on where the file came from.

138
00:13:26.350 --> 00:13:33.225
Software that averages them together will produce a number that means nothing at all, and such software exists.

139
00:13:33.225 --> 00:13:35.000
Read the header.

140
00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:37.425
The file says which it is.

